(* The package esp-now: packets between two ESP32 boards over ESP-NOW, the
   connectionless protocol of the Wi-Fi radio of the ESP32. Grades:
   time-interval-rational, in milliseconds.

   A packet carries a tag, a natural number, and two floats; their meaning is
   that of the application. The peer is the address given by the build flag
   ESP_NOW_PEER, six bytes separated by commas, other than the broadcast
   address, and the key is that given by the build flag ESP_NOW_KEY, 32
   hexadecimal digits, by which ESP-NOW encrypts and authenticates the
   packets with CCMP; both are required.

   LinkStart channel starts the radio as a station on the given channel, from
   1 to 13, and ESP-NOW with the peer, on core 0 whatever the core of the
   caller, and returns whether the link is encrypted, true for every link of
   the package; a second LinkStart, a channel outside 1 to 13 and a failure
   of the radio are run-time errors.

   Send (tag, first, second) queues one packet to the peer and returns without
   waiting for its transmission. A packet the radio cannot queue is dropped
   and counted. A Send before LinkStart is a run-time error.

   Receive age returns (true, tag, first, second), the latest packet received
   from the peer, if it arrived at most age milliseconds ago, and else
   (false, 0, 0, 0). It does not wait, and returns the same packet while it
   is not older than age. A packet from another address, of another format or
   with a float that is not finite is discarded.

   The bounds are provisional, not yet measured on a device: LinkStart
   [0, 1500], Send [0, 1], Receive [0, 0.1]. The default implementations take
   a time within the bounds, send nothing, receive nothing and start an
   encrypted link.

   On the profile m5stack-gray-radio, an M5Stack Gray, the radio on core 0,
   the tasks of the application on core 1 and the link encrypted, the
   profile of the device (2026-10-19) measured LinkStart at 88.500 to 95.847
   ms, 85.516 ms in a later run and 100.176 ms in a run at a period of 5 ms,
   and Receive at 0.002 to 0.078 ms, and at 0.003 to 0.387 ms in the run at
   5 ms. Their bounds there are the least time measured, rounded down, and
   1.5 times the greatest, rounded up: LinkStart [85.5, 144] and Receive
   [0, 0.6]. *)

operation LinkStart : nat ~> bool # [0, 1500]
default LinkStart _ = delay 100; true

operation Send : nat * float * float ~> unit # [0, 1]
default Send _ = delay 1/10

operation Receive : nat ~> bool * nat * float * float # [0, 1/10]
default Receive _ = delay 1/100; (false, 0, 0.0, 0.0)
